From 51c46bb130696e2a8818d736f60194c2dbf8b02a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 09:05:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] hs_cache: Add an off-by-default per-descriptor fetch
 rate limit

An HSDir queues the whole descriptor for every fetch it answers. When
fetches of a few descriptors flood an HSDir (tor#41339), there is no way
to bound the bandwidth they use.

Give each cached descriptor a fetch token bucket, and add
HSDirDescriptorFetchRate (default 5 per second) and
HSDirDescriptorFetchBurst (default 20). Fetches over the limit are
counted as tor_relay_dos_total{type="hsdir_fetch_over_rate"}. When the
new HSDirDescriptorFetchLimit option is set, the HSDir also closes the
circuit of such a fetch with END_CIRC_REASON_RESOURCELIMIT before
queuing the descriptor, as HSDirOneRequestPerCircuit does, and doesn't
count it as a success.

The bucket is shared by every client of a descriptor, so anyone can use
it up and deny that descriptor on this HSDir until it refills; clients
then fetch it from the descriptor's other HSDirs. That makes the limit
an operator's choice for saving bandwidth during a flood, not a
network-wide defense, so unlike the other HSDir defenses it has no
consensus parameters and is off by default. Popular services can also
legitimately exceed the default rate, which the counter shows.

Part of #41339.

